; ACCEPTANCE: a program that calls two libraries the target does not link by
; default BUILDS, and the host answers.
;
; Until linkline-2026-09-13 the windows link line was a constant naming kernel32,
; msvcrt, ucrt, vcruntime and legacy_stdio_definitions, so a generated
; declaration from anywhere else loaded, type-checked, assembled and then failed
; at the linker: `unresolved external symbol`. 3,427 of the 4,093 callable names
; were in that position and the survey counted every one of them callable.
;
; Now a declaration carries `(lib "…")` and the library joins the line only when
; a program calls it. This one calls two:
;
;   IsCharAlphaA       user32     'A' (65) is alphabetic, '1' (49) is not
;   GetSidLengthRequired  advapi32   8 + 4 bytes per sub-authority
;
; It must print 1, 0, 12, 28 — what a C program on this host prints, verified
; before this was written. FOUR ANSWERS FROM TWO LIBRARIES, and two per
; declaration, so neither call can be right by accident.
;
; And against the old link line it cannot build at all: the linker names both
; symbols as unresolved. The witness fails the build rather than the output,
; which is the failure the bug had.
;
; It needs `-checked` for the reason every Win32 acceptance program does:
; win/fmt.print-int does arithmetic on its argument, and a host call's result
; has no declared range.

(use win/WinUser as u)
(use win/securitybaseapi as s)
(use win/fmt as fmt)

(export main)

(def main (fn ()
  (seq (fmt.print-int (u.IsCharAlphaA 65))
       (fmt.print-int (u.IsCharAlphaA 49))
       (fmt.print-int (s.GetSidLengthRequired 1))
       (fmt.print-int (s.GetSidLengthRequired 5)))))
